  "personMemoryDetailsTitle": "How memory works",
  "@personMemoryDetailsTitle": {
    "description": "Title of the dialog with the full explanation of personal memory."
  },
  "personMemoryDetails": "Each chat, groups included, has a memory of its own: plans, topics, the tone you use there. Each person has a short one used in every chat they are in: name, relationship, birthday, formal or casual.\n\nWith automatic learning on, Sennit reads an active chat again after about 30 new messages, or once a day, while the app is open. It sends up to the last {count} messages to your AI model and updates the memory itself: it adds new facts, rewrites changed ones and drops stale ones. Each learned fact shows when it was learned. Edit, pin or delete it, or undo the last update; AI never changes a fact you edited or pinned.\n\nIt also learns how you write in each chat (language, length, emojis) and how the other person writes. Reply suggestions, quick messages and auto-replies are then written in your style, with a few of your recent messages in that chat as examples.\n\nWith personal memory on, every AI feature (reply suggestions, quick messages, auto-replies, to-dos and calendar titles) sends a shortened memory of the chat and its people with its request.\n\nMemory is stored only on this device, encrypted in the system keychain, not synced, included in backups and deleted with your account. Turn personal memory off and nothing is sent.",
